-- title:
--   Drinfeld chart as a flat, dense extension of the j-chart algebra
-- statement:
--   Fix primes $q \ge 5$ and $\ell \ge 3$ with $\ell \neq q$, and $M' \neq 0$ divisible by neither; a field $L$ of characteristic zero, a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity $\xi \in L$, and a ring homomorphism $L \to \mathbb{C}$ sending $\xi$ to $e^{2\pi i/(q\ell)}$. Let $K$ be the intermediate field of $L \subset \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ obtained by adjoining to $L$ the coefficientwise image of the $X_H$ function field of level $(q\ell)^2M'$ with $H$ the kernel of $(\mathbb{Z}/(q\ell)^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q\ell)^\times$. Let $A$ be a henselian discrete valuation ring with fraction field $L$, algebraically closed residue field, $q \in \mathfrak{m}_A = (\varpi)$, and $\xi$ in the image of $A$; let $j \in K$, $j \neq 0$, have Laurent expansion the image of the $j$-series, and $\varpi_t \in A$ satisfy $\varpi_t^{q^2-1} = q u$ with $u$ a unit. Write $C = \mathrm{chartAlgFin}\,A\,K\,j$ for the $A$-subalgebra of elements of $K$ integral over $A[j]$, and $\mathfrak{X}$ for the two-chart integral model (the pushout of the two chart maps). Let $y \subset C$ be maximal, containing $\varpi$, and supersingular in the sense that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring map $\varphi : C \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$, the image $\varphi(j)$ lies in $\mathrm{ssJSet}\,q\,\Omega$, i.e. every elliptic curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero $q$-torsion point. Let $z \in \mathfrak{X}$ be a point at which the image $\varpi_z$ of $\varpi$ under the structure map to $\mathrm{Spec}\,A$ lies in the maximal ideal of the stalk, let $y' \in \mathrm{Spec}\,C$ satisfy $\iota_{\mathrm{Fin}}(y') = z$, $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal} = y$, and the same supersingularity condition. Finally let $W_1$ be a complete discrete valuation domain, $\sigma_1 : A \to W_1$ a ring map with $\mathfrak{m}_{W_1} = (\sigma_1\varpi)$, let $u_1, v_1 \in W_1[[X_0,X_1]]$ be units and $f_1 \equiv X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1 \pmod{(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}}$, and let $e_1$ be a ring isomorphism from the $\mathfrak{m}$-adic completion of the stalk at $z$ onto $S = W_1[[X_0,X_1]]/(\sigma_1(\varpi_t^{q+1})v_1 - f_1u_1)$ carrying the germ of each constant $a \in A$ to the class of the constant $\sigma_1 a$. Put $\Psi : C \to S$ for the composite of the germ at $z$ along the image of the finite chart, the completion map and $e_1$, and $\mathfrak{M} = (\sigma_1\varpi, X_0, X_1) \subset S$. The conclusion asserts: $\Psi^{-1}(\mathfrak{M}) = y$; for every $n$ and every $s \in S$ there is $a \in C$ with $\Psi(a) - s \in \mathfrak{M}^n$; $\Psi(a) = \sigma_1(a)$ for $a \in A$; $C = A + y$; $W_1 = \sigma_1(A) + \mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$ and $\sigma_1^{-1}(\mathfrak{m}_{W_1}) = \mathfrak{m}_A$; $\mathfrak{M}$ is maximal and is the only maximal ideal of $S$; and $S$ is flat as a $C$-module via $\Psi$.
--
--   This is the dictionary relating the $j$-finite chart algebra of the integral model to an explicitly presented Drinfeld local chart at a supersingular point in characteristic $q$: the chart map is local, has dense image for the $\mathfrak{M}$-adic topology, is the identity on constants, induces an isomorphism on residue fields, and is flat. It is used throughout the subsequent analysis of the blow-up of the model at supersingular points, in particular in the identification of the exceptional components and of the decomposition and inertia action there.
